Dear Critics Johns, Idlout, McPherson, Bezan, Normandin, and Brunelle-Duceppe,

We are addressing this brief specifically to the Opposition defence and foreign affairs critics in the 45th Parliament, while CCing the relevant Ministers who received our primary disclosure on February 27, 2026. (For the newly added Parliamentary Critics, we have sent our February 27 disclosure outlining the systemic fraud and 46.8% wage discrimination practices inside the Hanwha Ocean supply chain to your offices in a separate email today). Now, we address an imminent threat to Canadian sovereignty.The MASGA Threat: Hanwha's Direct Integration with Donald Trump

In the same weeks Canadian officials are evaluating Hanwha Ocean for Canada's $60 billion submarine procurement, Donald Trump is openly threatening Canadian sovereignty, border security, and trade, including recent rhetoric regarding the annexation of Canada.

Simultaneously, Hanwha has structurally bound itself to Donald Trump's "Golden Fleet" naval and defense-industrial revitalization agenda.Direct US Penetration: In December 2025, Donald Trump personally named Hanwha in a Mar-a-Lago press conference as the preferred partner to build new US Navy frigates.The "MASGA" Framework: Hanwha acquired the Philadelphia Navy Shipyard in 2024 for $100M and pledged $5 billion to transform it. This is the keystone of the "Make American Shipbuilding Great Again" (MASGA) framework—a $150 billion South Korean shipbuilding commitment to the US.The Quid Pro Quo: In exchange for MASGA, the Trump administration slashed tariffs on Korean exports from 25% to 15% and approved South Korea's long-sought plan to build nuclear-powered submarines.

The Canadian Jeopardy: The Carney government is effectively positioning Canada to award its most strategic maritime defense asset ($60B) to the exact corporation Donald Trump has selected as his anchor for MASGA.

For the NDP and the Bloc Québécois—parties explicitly opposing US infringement on Canadian autonomy and Arctic sovereignty—a Hanwha victory means embedding Trump's preferred defense contractor into Canada’s northern patrol infrastructure. It invites profound sovereignty, intelligence, and geopolitical risks, handing Trump leverage over Canada’s key naval supply network.Evidence Already on the Table: ESG Failures & Forced Labour Law Violations

As detailed in yesterday's filing to Ministers McGuinty and Anand (which Global Affairs Canada has possessed in part since April 2025 without triggering any internal escalation under ATIP EA2025_0134154):Discriminatory Shipyards: At the very Geoje shipyard Stephen Fuhr visited on Feb 2, 2026, Hanwha Ocean paid subcontracted migrant workers bonuses equal to exactly 46.8% of what Korean workers received, violating public commitments and raising severe risks under Canada’s Fighting Against Forced Labour and Child Labour in Supply Chains Act.Fabricated Supply Chain "Talent": Hanwha's vaunted "technology transfer" relies on a Korean university pipeline that we have categorically exposed for systemic fraud. (Over 34 falsified international partnerships exposed; Dongguk University completely scrubbed UBC from its partner site upon FOI investigation). The 61.5% sexual violence rate (KWDI) experienced by female arts/media students within these institutions underscores an Epstein-equivalent exploitation environment.The 200,000 Jobs Illusion: The figure championed by Korea’s Foreign Minister in Ottawa is an un-audited "job-years" statistic sourced directly from Hanwha-sponsored lobbying content in The Hill Times.Call to Action for the Opposition

Minister McGuinty promised a "fair, arm's length" evaluation. A process cannot be "fair or arm's length" if it deliberately ignores widespread ESG non-compliance, active labour abuses, documented educational fraud in the vendor's talent pipeline, and the vendor's structural capture by a hostile US administration.

We ask the Official Opposition and critics to immediately request:A full national security impact assessment regarding Hanwha's integration into Trump's MASGA framework and how it compromises Canadian sovereignty.An independent supply chain audit of Hanwha Ocean under the Fighting Against Forced Labour and Child Labour in Supply Chains Act prior to any contract-signing or down-selection.

The evidence is fully accessible in our briefs linked above. We remain available to provide the primary source .eml and ATIP records to your parliamentary offices.

Sincerely,

Gender Watchdog Advocacy for Safe Campuses in Korea Supported by End Rape On Campus (EROC)

